  <entry>
    <title>Why Midscene&#39;s Action Space Is a Protocol</title>
    <link href="http://quanru.github.io/2026/05/27/Action-Space-as-a-Protocol/"/>
    <id>http://quanru.github.io/2026/05/27/Action-Space-as-a-Protocol/</id>
    <published>2026-05-27T20:00:00.000Z</published>
    <updated>2026-08-07T11:30:12.341Z</updated>
    
    
    <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;How many places do you have to touch to add a new action to Midscene?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is my rough-and-ready test for whether a framework is well designed. If adding a “pull to refresh” gesture means changing the locating logic, changing the planning prompt, changing the execution dispatch, and then patching in a pile of if-else along the way, then sooner or later the framework gets crushed under its own action space.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Midscene’s answer is: you add it in one place. This post is about how it does that, and about the part it does not do.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;/post-img/action-space-protocol-en.svg&quot; alt=&quot;The action space as a Zod protocol&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
    
    
    
