  <entry>
    <title>Why Does Midscene&#39;s UI Agent Need to See the Screen?</title>
    <link href="http://quanru.github.io/2026/05/26/Why-Does-a-UI-Agent-Need-to-See-the-Screen/"/>
    <id>http://quanru.github.io/2026/05/26/Why-Does-a-UI-Agent-Need-to-See-the-Screen/</id>
    <published>2026-05-26T12:43:23.000Z</published>
    <updated>2026-08-07T11:30:12.344Z</updated>
    
    
    <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;While working on Midscene, I often run into the same question: why does a UI Agent need screenshots? Why not keep using DOM, selectors, XPath, accessibility trees, and the other things traditional automation has already made mature?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is a fair question. For more than a decade, UI automation has mostly grown around structured interface data. But if we are not trying to build just a smarter Web testing framework, and instead want a UI Agent that can operate Web pages, mobile apps, desktop apps, Canvas, and custom devices, the default input has to shift a little: see the screen first, then decide what to do.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;/post-img/ui-agent-screen-first-cover-en.svg&quot; alt=&quot;A UI Agent should see the screen first&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
    
    
    
